You can teach `cast` how to convert a struct or choice you declare. For
example, a label can supply its text whenever a cast asks for a String.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Label = | text String |

    to_string |this Label| -> String {
        return this.text
    }

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING

    label = Label("Settings")
    caption String = cast label
]

The annotation on `caption` sets the target. The method supplies the text
and the `must` declaration tells Moth to use that method for this conversion.
Here the cast always succeeds and `caption` holds `"Settings"`.

A conversion that can fail uses the fallible trait for its target. The
method then returns either the converted value or a builtin `Error`. The
caller still writes `cast` and can use `catch` to supply a fallback. See
@../casts/#fallible-casts (Fallible casts).
